 "arm_in_words": "NOT the deployed route. This is the route reader's OWN code at the base commit (holdings_gold.calculate and map_account, with the source the route composes at installed.py:75 for each of its two firms) run locally over the SERVED holdings gold read from S3 (list and get only; no Lambda invoked, no sign-in, no Secrets Manager read, nothing written to AWS). Every partition was read ONCE from S3 into a local cache outside the worktree; the reader's calculate() was then run once per account per window, with the account's rows served from memory by a stand-in for HoldingsGoldSource's transport (partition_dates, account_keys, frame, _read). What the reader computes from the rows (the account mapping, the filter at holdings_gold.py:246, the chain at :253-258, days_excluded at :274, the claim at :291/:295, the absences at :308-341) is the reader's own code. Two things are not the reader's: the stand-in transport, and ids.opaque_account memoised with functools.lru_cache (same function, same outputs; map_account calls it once per account per call). Checked on a sample of 50 accounts: the result with the stand-in equals the result with the reader's OWN HoldingsGoldSource class (partition_dates, frame and account_keys real; only the S3 get and parquet parse of _read replaced by the cached frame); see transport_check. The position classes, the distribution and the status tallies are counted by this driver from the same cached rows (classify() in ib160_measure.py), and the reader's days_excluded was compared with the driver's own count of non-OK rows for every account that got a figure (field reader_days_excluded_differs_from_status_count_of_rows). What could differ from the deployed function: its packaged bytes and dependencies, its mapping loader, the route's authorization and scope resolution (the scope is built in memory, one permitted account), and its response envelope; the reader's answer dict is read, not the route's HTTP body.",
